Levi Stubbs delivers much of the song in a tone that straddles the line between singing and shouting,<ref name="pc50"/> as he did on the group's 1965 hit "[[I Can't Help Myself (Sugar Pie Honey Bunch)]]". [[AllMusic]] critic Ed Hogan praises Stubbs' vocals, as well as the song's "rock-solid groove" and "dramatic, semi-operatic tension and release."<ref>{{cite web|title= Reach Out (I'll Be There) – Song Review |publisher= [[AllMusic]] |access-date= July 14, 2014 |url= http://www.allmusic.com/song/reach-out-ill-be-there-mt0010884565}}</ref> Critic Martin Charles Strong calls the song "a soul symphony of epic proportions that remains [the Four Tops'] signature tune."<ref>{{cite book|first= Martin Charles |last= Strong |author-link= Martin C. Strong |year= 2004 |title= The Great Rock Discography |publisher= Canongate |page= 556 |isbn= 978-1-8419-5615-2}}</ref>

In 2014, Fakir stated in an interview with ''[[The Guardian]]'':

{{blockquote|Eddie [Holland] realized that when Levi hit the top of his vocal range, it sounded like someone hurting, so he made him sing right up there. Levi complained, but we knew he loved it. Every time they thought he was at the top, he would reach a little further until you could hear the tears in his voice. The line "Just look over your shoulder" was something he threw in spontaneously. Levi was creative like that; he could always add something from the heart.<ref>{{cite news|first= Dave |last=  Simpson |title= The Four Tops: how we made Reach Out (I'll Be There) |newspaper= [[The Guardian]] |date= April 7, 2014 |access-date= December 16, 2014 |url= https://www.theguardian.com/music/2014/apr/07/how-we-made-reach-out-four-tops}}</ref>}}

